ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Inflammatory Bowel Disease Crohn's - Articular - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Septic Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Polyarticular presentation temporally linked to a known gastrointestinal flare, lacking high-grade spiking fever.

Mechanical Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Active passage of diarrhea and flatus, with a soft, non-rigid abdomen.

Gout - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Atypical polyarticular large-joint distribution and direct chronologic association with active IBD exacerbation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The simultaneous presentation of severe polyarticular immobility and active gastrointestinal volume loss required an immediate physical evaluation to exclude a surgical abdomen and joint sepsis. Delaying care would have subjected a highly vulnerable, immobilized patient to unacceptable pain, worsening dehydration, and potential traumatic falls while attempting to reach sanitary facilities. Urgent administration of parenteral corticosteroids and antiemetics was required to chemically halt the dual-system inflammatory cascade in the home environment.

